WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.302
Are these two situations equivalent?

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.204
On the left, you're sitting on the Earth.

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.040
On the right, you're inside an accelerating rocket.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.045
Either way, you feel your body pushing against the floor as if there's gravity.

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:14.514
It's impossible for you to tell the difference.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.648
Or is it?

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:18.752
[Intro Music]

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.053
Hey Crazies.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:23.623
What we're talking about here is something called the “equivalence principle.”

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.725
If you're accelerating uniformly,

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.962
any measurements you take will be physically equivalent

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:34.034
to measurements taken in a uniform gravitational field of appropriate strength.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:37.203
It's one of the founding principles of general relativity.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:38.238
But is it true?

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:41.274
And if it's not, does that make relativity invalid?

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.710
We'll come back to both of these questions a little later.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.847
For now, I just want you to imagine you've woken up, locked in a room.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.816
This room has no windows.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.251
You hear no sound.

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:53.086
You don't feel any vibrations.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:56.022
You're completely isolated from the world outside.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:59.626
This is exactly the situation I've put one of my expendable clones in.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.795
Great. Here we go again.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.797
This is going to be hilarious.

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:05.098
Where do you think you are, man?

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.067
Well, I can't tell exactly you,

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.102
but I feel my weight against the floor,

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.571
so I'm going to guess this room is somewhere on Earth.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.306
Interesting. Interesting.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:15.408
What if I told you you weren't on Earth?

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:18.211
Oh, so I'm on another planet with the same gravity?

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:18.812
Nope.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:23.716
You're inside a rocket accelerating at exactly 9.8 meters per second squared.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.086
Weird. I can't tell the difference.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.655
That's exactly the point.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:33.660
The 9.8 meters per second squared is what we call the “acceleration due to gravity,”

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.694
on Earth at least.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:39.199
It's a gain of 9.8 meters per second for every second to travel.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:46.039
For some perspective, 9.8 meters per second is about 22 miles per hour or 35 kilometers per hour.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:51.077
Without air resistance, that's how much a falling object speeds up every second.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:56.883
For Expendable Clone to feel his weight against the floor, his rocket must share that acceleration.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:02:01.521
It's accelerating through deep space at the same rate that things fall toward Earth.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:06.259
What most of us call “weight” is just the feeling of weight, not the weight itself.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:11.197
Right now, you're feeling an upward push from the ground or your chair or both.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.901
You also have a weight pulling you down, something you never feel.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:20.607
Sure, that upward push only exists because the Earth is already pulling you down with gravity,

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.710
but what if there was another reason for that upward push?

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:27.080
Like a rocket you happen to be inside, accelerating forward.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.949
You would feel a weight even though you don't have one.

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:35.889
But, just because one situation is indistinguishable from another, that doesn't necessarily make them equivalent.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:41.394
There may be an upward push in both situations, but only one has a weight. Right?

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:45.565
We're not saying his contact with the floor is gravity, are we?

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:49.202
The equivalence principle seems to say so, but that's kind of sus.

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:54.174
And a huge part of science is tearing ideas to shreds for the benefit of all humankind.

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:58.344
Whatever survives is the truth, or at least approximately the truth.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.247
So let's see if we can find an exception to this rule.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:04.184
What if we bring in another object, say, a squirrel?

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:08.855
From the comfort of our space station, we see a squirrel floating stationary in space.

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:13.826
As the rocket accelerates upward, the squirrel slips through a hole and into the rocket.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:19.699
From Expendable Clone’s point of view, it would be as if a squirrel fell from the sky into his room.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.868
It's a perfectly reasonable conclusion.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.670
Not only does the squirrel fall,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:28.107
but it falls in exactly the same way that it would if it were falling on Earth.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.710
It's as if there's a gravity acting on the squirrel,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.680
even though we know there's no source of gravity nearby.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.015
The equivalence principle still seems to hold up.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:39.919
Hmm. What if we have the squirrel come in from the side instead?

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:43.289
Let's say the squirrel is moving in a straight line sideways.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:48.228
As the rocket accelerates upward, the squirrel slips through a hole in the side of the rocket.

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:52.198
Expendable clone will see the squirrel enter his room from the wall

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:55.468
and then fall to the floor in an arc as if there was gravity.

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:59.706
If we could see inside his room, we'd see the squirrel still moving in a straight line.

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.908
It would be the floor accelerating upward.

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:06.579
But, according to my clone, it's the squirrel that accelerates downward like a projectile.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.748
To him, it looks like there's gravity.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:11.184
The equivalence principle still holds.

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.686
I think it's time for something a little more extreme.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:17.757
Let's say his rocket is like, 20 miles long, and he's running up the stairs.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:21.995
Every ten floors or so, he stops to measure his weight with a bathroom scale.

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.732
No matter what floor he's on, his weight will always be the same.

00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:28.401
That wouldn't be true with a 20 mile tall building.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:32.372
In that case, your weight would gradually decrease as you went up floors.

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:36.843
Your weight on the top floor would be 1% less than your weight on the bottom floor.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:39.445
Bingo Bango! Equivalence Principle broken.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:42.215
(Off Camera Voice) Wait a minute. That's not a uniform gravitational field.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.516
Oh, crap.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:46.519
This principle does require that gravity be uniform.

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:49.522
And that's only approximately true near the surface of the earth.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:53.559
If you get far enough away, we stop being able to make that approximation.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:57.764
Not only does gravity get weaker with distance, it can also turn.

00:04:57.764 --> 00:05:02.302
Down is toward the center of the earth, which isn't always the same direction.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.338
Earth is round and sometimes we have to account for that.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.507
Really long bridges are a good example.

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:11.444
If we were to drop two squirrels a mile apart and from a mile high,

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:16.783
they'd be 1.3 feet or 40 centimeters closer together by the time they hit the ground,

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:20.887
which is yet another situation where the equivalence principle does not apply.

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:23.856
If the accelerating rocket was a one mile cube,

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:28.061
the two squirrels would stay exactly one mile apart the entire fall.

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:34.200
The equivalence principle only compares uniform acceleration with uniform gravitational fields,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.536
but it was never meant to be a universal law.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:41.541
Its purpose is to help us design thought experiments, so we can ask the right questions.

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:47.480
The experiments in this video seem to suggest that gravity is simply acceleration we can't see.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.851
Since they only happen in our minds, they can't tell us if that conclusion is true.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:55.988
But if it is true, it can tell us what consequences we might see.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:59.892
Remember when we had a squirrel enter Expendable Clone’s room through a wall?

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:03.162
Well, what would happen if a pulse of light entered the room instead?

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:06.199
It would also bend toward the floor like the squirrels path did,

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:07.700
just not nearly as much.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.603
The bend would be subtle because light is fast.

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:11.604
Fast Fast!

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:13.639
If we believe the equivalence principle,

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.608
and we haven't found any reason not to,

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:18.478
then that means light should be affected by gravity,

00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:19.712
just like squirrels.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:21.814
Which seems absolutely bonkers.

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:26.519
There's a reason people didn't believe Einstein and Hilbert when they published this in 1915.

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:30.723
They had to actually prove it, which means they had to wait for a total solar eclipse

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.625
and for a World War 1 to end.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:37.063
Europe was leading science at the time, and they were understandably kind of a mess.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:42.702
Anyway, the idea is that if light from a star passes close enough to the Sun on its way to Earth,

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:48.441
then the Sun will bend its path, making it appear in a slightly different location in the sky.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:50.009
And I mean slightly.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:54.013
They estimated the deflection angle to be less than two arcseconds.

00:06:54.013 --> 00:06:57.350
That's less than an 1800th of a degree.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:02.255
The U.S. tried to do the experiment in June of 1918 in Baker City, Oregon.

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:03.756
That was near the end of the war.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.393
But, alas, it was cloudy with a 100% chance of failure.

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:11.497
The next total solar eclipse wouldn't happen until May of 1919.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:14.500
European scientists planned out two expeditions:

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.505
one for several Brazil in South America and the other for Príncipe in Africa.

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:22.942
The hope was that at least one of them would get decent images.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:26.546
And, after some technical difficulties, both teams managed it.

00:07:26.546 --> 00:07:28.681
Here's one of the images from Brazil.

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:33.219
The results of this experiment confirmed that prediction from general relativity.

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:36.923
Light is, in fact, affected by gravity, just like squirrels.

00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:41.461
As far as we can tell, gravity is just acceleration we can't see.

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:44.764
The equivalence principle might not apply to solar eclipses.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:47.099
But it did present us with a thought experiment,

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.868
which led us to conduct a real one.

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:52.205
A real experiment we never would have thought to do otherwise.

00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:55.007
So is the equivalence principle true? Yes.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:56.943
Is it universal? No.

00:07:56.976 --> 00:08:00.146
But that doesn't mean it hasn't been extremely useful.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.850
And until next time, remember, it's okay to be a little crazy.

